WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.390
Horrific dives into the past, modernist AI
nightmares, and so much more.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:09.590
With "Black Mirror" Season 6 available to
watch now on Streamberry — er, we mean,

00:00:09.590 --> 00:00:14.099
Netflix — here's an explanation of all the
twisted endings.

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:17.970
Joan seems to have it all — everything,
that is, except control over her own life.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.080
A nice apartment, a loving boyfriend, and
a position of authority at her workplace,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.349
but none of it feels like something she chose.

00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:29.259
When she is forced to fire a dedicated employee
and evades responsibility for it, her morale

00:00:29.259 --> 00:00:30.630
couldn't be any lower.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:37.040
That is, until she sees a show seemingly targeted
directly at her on Streamberry titled "Joan is Awful."

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:38.040
"Well, we're watching it."

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.789
"No, we're not watching 'Joan is Awful,' whatever
the hell it is."

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:43.130
"Just hit play."

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:47.690
Curious at first, and then horrified, she
discovers that "Joan is Awful" is based directly

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:49.789
on her and her actual life.

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:54.329
Joan immediately spirals as she sees all of
her worst moments slightly exaggerated for

00:00:54.329 --> 00:00:58.960
dramatic purposes in the capable hands of
the person playing Joan, Salma Hayek.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.010
With the current anxiety around AI and ChatGPT
potentially replacing creatives, "Joan is

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.159
Awful" couldn't have dropped at a better time.

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:10.119
Joan is forced to learn that she has signed
away her likeness to Streamberry simply by

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:13.909
signing their massive user agreement when
she first subscribed.

00:01:13.909 --> 00:01:17.420
She teams up with Salma Hayek — who signed
over the rights for her own likeness to be

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.270
used as a digital creation in the show — to
get back at the people that have tarnished

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.329
their images.

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:26.439
Storming the Streamberry HQ, they discover
that a quantum computer has been using AI

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:28.840
to render these targeted reality shows.

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:33.827
And in the big twist, they find that their
reality is in turn a show for the original Joan.

00:01:33.827 --> 00:01:35.310
"Source Joan lives in reality.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.870
When Source Joan watches the TV show 'Joan
is Awful' she sees you playing her.

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:41.820
That show is the fictive level we're on right
now, here."

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.338
The episode has a happy ending by the time
the credits roll, but it doesn't reduce the message.

00:01:46.338 --> 00:01:50.960
By preying on relevant fears of intrusive
technology and the worrying question of whether

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.210
even our private lives are secret, "Joan is
Awful" takes a meta approach to storytelling

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.549
that will make most viewers squirm.

00:01:58.549 --> 00:01:59.990
But there's also hope.

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:03.460
Joan ultimately takes control of her own life
rather than worry about what others expect

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:08.596
of her, and we see the quantum computer destroyed
and Joan back in the captain's seat of her reality.

00:02:08.596 --> 00:02:13.330
In and of itself, the world of true crime
can feel like a "Black Mirror" episode.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:17.569
Novice journalists with good intentions, a
desire for fame, and an unwarranted level

00:02:17.569 --> 00:02:22.310
of faith in justice contribute to a massively
popular genre that is regularly criticized

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.350
for retraumatizing survivors of violent crime.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:30.019
While there's often nuance to the conversation
in real life, it isn't nuance that filmmakers

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:34.430
Pia and Davis in "Loch Henry" are looking
for when they start digging into local lore

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:35.900
around Iain Adair .

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:40.280
Iain was a serial killer who lured tourists
into his basement to be tortured.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.870
He ultimately wounded Davis' father and, according
to his mother, Janet, caused health problems

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.200
that led to Davis' father's eventual death.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:49.970
Yet, things are not all as they seem.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.890
While Davis is in the hospital recovering
from a car crash, Pia pulls out one of Janet's

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.599
VHS tapes labeled "Bergerac," a long-running
British crime show from the 1980s focused

00:02:58.599 --> 00:03:01.170
on particularly unsettling cases.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:05.670
On the tape, instead of an old BBC detective
drama, she discovers horrifying footage of

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:09.730
Davis' parents torturing a couple to death
with Iain Adair.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:15.010
"Ken gave me that.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:18.170
We had such fun together."

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:22.940
Pia attempts to flee but suffers a fatal injury,
while Janet, who knows her secret is out,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:27.010
dies by suicide, leaving the rest of her gruesome
home movies for her son.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:32.110
Though Davis' documentary wins awards, the
lens of true crime has been refocused on him.

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:36.349
Alone, with the most painful things that have
ever happened to him up for public consumption,

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:40.530
Davis has gotten what he wanted but paid a
terrible price for it.

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:44.610
David and Cliff are astronauts in deep space,
with technology that allows them to transport

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:49.220
their consciousness into robotic forms back
on Earth to help them cope with the lengthy

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:50.220
time away.

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:54.590
When David's Earth robot is destroyed and
his family killed by a cult that's convinced

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:59.049
mechanical life is an abomination, Cliff and
his wife Lana invite David to use Cliff's

00:03:59.049 --> 00:04:01.709
link for an hour every week to help him cope.

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:04.340
Of course, that situation starts getting weird.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.540
David develops a crush on Lana, who rejects
him, but Cliff's desire to control Lana, their

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:13.170
son, and even David's grief process leads
to tragedy when David returns to take Cliff's

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.920
family away, just as he lost his own.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:20.777
Having it all might make a person feel invincible,
but, in the end, we can't control what life takes.

00:04:20.777 --> 00:04:24.970
Cliff's arrogant belief that he can control
the situation blows up in his face and, with

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:28.850
his power gone, he and David become true equals
for the first time.

00:04:28.850 --> 00:04:33.030
Though the episode ends on an open-ended note
with David kicking a chair to Cliff and inviting

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:37.310
him to metaphorically and literally sit at
the same table, it's clear that neither has

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.020
anything left to live for.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.930
Whether they continue on or don't no longer
matters.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.780
Whatever they do, they'll have to do it together.

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:49.470
Set in 2006, "Mazey Day" takes on classic
horror genre tropes by matching the hunger

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:53.900
of a werewolf with the unscrupulous behavior
of mid-aughts paparazzi.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:59.060
Protagonist Bo takes a photo that ruins an
actor's life and leads to his death by suicide.

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.790
While she's told this is not her fault, it
forces her to leave the business.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:06.640
That is, until the starlet Mazey Day vanishes
off the face of the planet after leaving a

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:09.870
film mid-shoot, driving up interest in photos
of her.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:13.660
After following her to a rehab clinic, Bo
and her fellow photographers are shocked to

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.009
discover Mazey Day isn't suffering from standard
celebrity dramas — rather, she has been

00:05:18.009 --> 00:05:20.600
transformed into a werewolf.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.600
"We gotta go." 

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:22.743
"Just a couple more."

00:05:27.789 --> 00:05:31.590
Though Bo and her peers attempt to flee, the
werewolf makes short work of them until Bo

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.710
is able to get in a single shot that grounds
the beast.

00:05:34.710 --> 00:05:40.150
Bloody, naked, and weeping, Mazey Day begs
Bo to kill her, but Bo opts instead to hand

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:43.250
her a gun so that she can catch it all on
film.

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:47.330
With Mazey crying and holding a gun to her
head and Bo's finger hovering over the shutter,

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:52.020
the parallel between these two very different
kinds of "shooting" couldn't be clearer.

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.710
While this might seem like a departure for
the series, the fact is that there is nothing

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:59.889
more "Black Mirror" than commentary on the
toxicity of paparazzi culture.

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:04.259
Indeed, this episode only pulls overarching
themes together more than ever by revealing

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:09.389
that humanity's various dystopias can't be
blamed on technology alone.

00:06:09.389 --> 00:06:15.580
Taking place in England 1979, "Demon 79" centers
on Nida, a South Asian woman working in a

00:06:15.580 --> 00:06:16.580
shoe store.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:20.460
She's regularly forced to deal with racist
aggression as her coworker expresses open

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:22.729
resentment toward her and her culture.

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:27.430
Nida copes through violent fantasies of destruction
and mayhem but leads a relatively quiet and

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:28.800
unassuming life.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.080
That is, until she accidentally splashes a
rune with her own blood, inadvertently summoning

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.850
the demon Gaap, who tells her that she needs
to kill three people to avoid the apocalypse.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:39.400
"Let's stop it happening, you and me.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.400
All we have to do is deliver three sacrifices
in three days.

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:46.099
It's only three killings."

00:06:46.099 --> 00:06:51.440
Noting that there is a surplus of truly reprehensible
people surrounding Nida, Gaap coaxes her along

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:53.200
as she targets who she will kill.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:58.080
A well-meaning policeman stops her just short
of her goal, and, well, just as Gaap promised,

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.180
the apocalypse happens.

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:04.070
Still, things aren't all bad as Gaap invites
Nida along to endure nothingness with him,

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:07.289
implying that it might not be so bad if at
least they're not alone.

00:07:07.289 --> 00:07:12.930
While "Demon 79" is a farce based on nuclear
anxiety, late '70s xenophobia, and unaddressed

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:17.740
mental health issues, its heart shows us a
bleak level of horror where even basically

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:21.479
"good" acts might not save us from humanity's
worst in the end.

00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:25.380
While Nida has a relatively happy ending,
there is no denying that her time on Earth

00:07:25.380 --> 00:07:30.000
in this episode is majorly defined by other
people's racism, without which neither the

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.069
murders nor the apocalypse would happen.

00:07:33.069 --> 00:07:37.760
This season of "Black Mirror" features several
Easter eggs and references to past episodes.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.479
The most notable of which hinge on its in-universe
streaming giant, Streamberry — a stand-in

00:07:42.479 --> 00:07:47.639
for Netflix itself — from the catchy name
down to the flashy logo and user interface.

00:07:47.639 --> 00:07:52.110
In "Joan is Awful," there is a moment of foreshadowing
for the following episode when the documentary

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:57.525
"Loch Henry: Truth Will Out" appears alongside
a number of other self-referential films and shows.

00:07:57.525 --> 00:08:01.270
These include documentaries like "Finding
Ritman," calling back to the lead antagonist

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:05.789
of "Bandersnatch," and "The Callow Years,"
a nod to Michael Callow from the first episode,

00:08:05.789 --> 00:08:07.039
"The National Anthem."

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:11.600
Besides a handful of other Streamberry in-jokes,
there are a few references to the White Bear

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:15.159
symbol that first appears in the Season 2
episode of the same name.

00:08:15.159 --> 00:08:20.270
First, in "Beyond the Sea," the anti-robotics
cult members that attack David's family scrawl

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:22.449
the symbol in blood on a wall.

00:08:22.449 --> 00:08:26.900
And in "Demon 79," the symbol appears on the
rune that Nida finds and accidentally splashes

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:28.139
with a drop of her blood.

00:08:28.139 --> 00:08:32.360
During her vision of the future, politician
Michael Smart's Britannia party logo also

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:35.349
bears a striking similarity to the mysterious
glyph.

00:08:35.349 --> 00:08:36.680
You probably recognize him.

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:38.310
He's all set to be our next MP."

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:42.360
"Well, with a little luck and a following
wind."

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:46.950
Speaking of Smart, his name appears in a news
scroll in "Loch Henry" that announces his

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:52.149
robot police dogs, foreshadowing the bleak
future seen in the Season 4 episode "Metalhead."

00:08:52.149 --> 00:08:56.700
Meanwhile, the TV series "Sea of Tranquility,"
another recurring "Black Mirror" reference,

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.720
makes an appearance in "Joan is Awful" as
one of the shows available for streaming.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:05.320
And in "Mazey Day," Bo outs the star of "Sea
of Tranquility," Justin Camley, by taking

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:07.730
photos of him meeting a lover at a hotel.

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:11.800
These aren't nearly all the references to
prior episodes to be found in Season 6, and

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:16.110
for some fans, part of the fun of "Black Mirror"
is trying to catch them all.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:20.060
As always, the anthology format of "Black
Mirror" explores the interactions between

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:23.040
technology and humanity taken to their extremes.

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:27.530
This season achieves this by shaking up the
timeline a bit more than it has before, implementing

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:32.300
just as many nods to modern life as ever while
also delving into the past in three of its

00:09:32.300 --> 00:09:33.529
five episodes.

00:09:33.529 --> 00:09:37.380
While "Joan is Awful" shows a woman forced
to see the immediate echo of everything she

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:42.269
does on a TV screen, "Loch Henry" warns of
the potential hazards of dredging up the past,

00:09:42.269 --> 00:09:47.024
followed by three episodes that are trips
to the yesteryear of the "Black Mirror" shared universe.

00:09:47.024 --> 00:09:51.810
By starting with a tale set so firmly in the
present, as many "Black Mirror" episodes are,

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:56.660
then using the last episodes to travel backward
as the show never has before, this season

00:09:56.660 --> 00:10:01.440
pointedly refuses nostalgia for any perceived
"good old days" before technology came along

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.750
and ruined everything.

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:07.050
Instead, it fleshes out a central theme that
technology is essentially benign and it's

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:11.380
the hand in which it rests that determines
whether it achieves good or ill.

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:15.399
It's important to note that characters that
are against technology run amok, such as the

00:10:15.399 --> 00:10:18.839
cult in "Beyond the Sea," are completely detached
from reality.

00:10:18.839 --> 00:10:22.870
This adds an overall sense that learning from
our mistakes with tech might be a healthier

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:25.990
approach than denying its benefits outright.

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:30.250
Much of this season revolves around a slightly
more optimistic view that, even in the worst

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:33.580
possible outcomes, there is always a silver
lining somewhere.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:37.900
As of this video, Netflix has not officially
greenlit a Season 7 of "Black Mirror," but

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:42.020
considering the ongoing hype around the series,
it seems unlikely that the plug will be pulled

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:43.020
anytime soon.

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:49.860
"It confirmed their innermost fears and put
them in a state of mesmerized horror, which

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.250
really drives engagement."

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:55.810
Series creator Charlie Brooker told Tudum
that any season of the series will have to

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:59.620
subvert expectations, even if that means subverting
his own at times.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.250
"I've always felt that Black Mirror should
feature stories that are entirely distinct

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:07.290
from one another, and keep surprising people
— and myself — or else what's the point?

00:11:07.290 --> 00:11:12.250
It should be a series that can't be easily
defined and can keep reinventing itself."

00:11:12.250 --> 00:11:17.243
With six seasons in the bag, this approach
is clearly what's kept fans coming back for more.

